{
 "_note": "TIER 2 as data: an `on:draw` script \u2192 the recorded COMMAND STREAM. This corpus is EXACT on all three renderers because it asserts the stream a renderer replays, never the pixels it produces. THE LAWS. (1) The CTM is baked into each path point AT POINT-ADD TIME \u2014 the Canvas2D rule, not the SVG one: translating after moveTo moves only the points added afterwards, and a scaled arc is an ellipse. A draw entry still carries the CTM in force (`ctm`) because stroke width, text and images scale with it. (2) `save`/`restore` push and pop the matrix AND the paint state (fill \u00b7 stroke \u00b7 lineWidth \u00b7 cap \u00b7 join \u00b7 globalAlpha \u00b7 shadow \u00b7 blend \u00b7 clip \u00b7 font) as one unit; restoring an empty stack is ONE diagnostic and a no-op, never a crash. (3) Angles: `arc` takes RADIANS (the Canvas2D signature authors know), `rotate` takes DEGREES (every other DSX angle). That split is deliberate and pinned here so it cannot drift. `arc` joins from the current point with a line, sweeps clockwise unless anticlockwise is set, clamps at a full turn, and folds to cubics through the same converter as `d`. (4) A draw with an EMPTY path emits nothing at all. (5) `measureText` answers from the FALLBACK metrics (advance 0.6\u00b7size, ascent 0.8\u00b7size, descent 0.2\u00b7size) whenever the platform supplies no real font metrics \u2014 pinned so the fallback itself cannot drift; a platform WITH metrics answers from them and that divergence is documented, not silent. (6) An unknown method or a missing argument is ONE diagnostic and the call is skipped \u2014 Article 7 all the way down.",
